  <title>Troubleshooting</title>
  <desc>What to do when a backup fails or Delta does not respond.</desc>

  <section id="locked">
    <title>The backup repository is locked</title>
    <p>A backup that was interrupted can leave the repository locked. Choose <gui style="button">Unlock…</gui>.</p>
    <note style="warning">
      <p>Continue only if no backup is running right now, on this computer or on any other that uses this repository. Unlocking a
      repository that is in use can damage your backups. Delta never does it by itself.</p>
    </note>
  </section>

  <section id="failed">
    <title>The last backup failed</title>
    <p>Choose <gui style="button">Try again</gui>. If it fails again, the message says why. The usual causes:</p>
    <list>
      <item><p><em>The passphrase of your backups is not in the keyring.</em> The window offers <gui>Enter passphrase…</gui>: type your passphrase,
      or your recovery key as in <link xref="recovery-key"/>. Delta checks it against your backups before keeping it in the keyring.</p></item>
      <item><p><em>Timed out waiting for the keyring. Is it locked?</em> Unlock it (log in again) and try again.</p></item>
      <item><p><em>The keyring service is not available.</em> Start your keyring, for example GNOME Keyring, in your session.</p></item>
      <item><p><em>The backup destination could not be created.</em> Check that the folder or drive is connected and that you can write to it.</p></item>
    </list>
  </section>

  <section id="service">
    <title>The backup service is not available</title>
    <p>Log out and back in. If it persists, run <cmd>journalctl --user -u delta</cmd> and look for the cause.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="gone">
    <title>That backup no longer exists</title>
    <p>A newer backup may have replaced it. Reopen the window to see the current ones.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="schedule">
    <title>Nothing happens on a schedule</title>
    <p>Check that <gui>Back up automatically</gui> in <gui style="menuitem">Preferences</gui> has the schedule you want, and that your
    systemd user session is running.</p>
  </section>
</page>
